A||||4||MrPBody||No brake booster?||||5||
Z||000000||MrPBody||01-29-2001||07:45 PM||||Ogre's post <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007762.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007762.html</A>  makes me wonder:<P>If we find the "right" brake master cylinder with quick take-up and a small working bore, can we do without the booster?  Would this improve brake pedal firmness and sensitivity?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||01-29-2001||07:58 PM||||I had taken that into account actually. I theory we could do just that. Problem is it's not a simple matter of eliminating the booster. Little things like finding a push rod and making sure it's linged up right are a pain. A small misalignment will wear the MC real quick as the pistons take side load.<P>We're also talking about a small bore change. 25.something to 24mm presently. This should give us more pressure but probably not enough increase to eliminate the booster. If we got any smaller I think we'll have a volume problem.<P>I've driven allot of cars. to be honest Fiero is a pretty firm pedal. The booster we have seems to be a much better match to things than allot of cars have. If yours is feeling mushy you could have a vacuum problem or the booster itself might need work. <P>One thing to check is the booster filter in the engine bay if you have one. If that's blocked you'll have problems. Also check the plastic valve on the booster shell. make sure it works and isn't leaking at all. If you have to replace the check valve or filter you can find them in the MotorMite Master Catalog at most parts stores. The valve is sometimes in MotorMite's red Help! packages.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||01-29-2001||08:30 PM||||Fiero's pedal does feel pretty good, in the context of vacuum-assisted systems.<P>I have driven many older cars -- British stuff, mostly -- with front disk brakes and no booster.  Typically, their brakes feel like a rock, and they stop well.  But probably an all-disk system demands significantly greater fluid volume.  I can't think of a car with 4-wheel disks without some kind of brake servo.<P>My '85 SE stops much better with new front brake pads.  I'll post about them after more miles.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||ray b||01-29-2001||09:34 PM|||| i would like more boost bigger diameter booster fit?or ?<BR>cant understand how less is more on master cyl. unless stroke is longer in 24mm should move less volume than 25+mm master cyl.if = sized =pressure. smaller piston = less volume larger= more volume. slave is bigger than master? 45 vs 25 but slave moves much less stroke ,master moves much more.seems backwards, where is advantage in this looks to me like loss not gain to be smaller in master cyl.but less leg work to push on brake petal. I dont understand   ray||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-29-2001||10:36 PM||||Smaller MC = less volume for the same stroke but more power. basic hydraulics.<P>If the new MC is to much smaller we won't get enough volume.<P>to make this more confusing we are dealing with "step bore" MC which doesn't work quite the same. We won't know for sure until someone gets time to test it.||||reg||1
